				</div>
				<p class="cas-note">
					Bandit-style "try and learn" controllers over length arms cannot beat this, because a stationary
					bandit learns the best single arm and therefore ties best-fixed by construction: 2.4015 against
					2.4022, with the contextual entropy stop at 2.6715. Acceptance history is already in the
					pre-round baseline for the same reason. <a href="/bandit-vs-signal">See the interactive demo →</a>
				</p>

				<h3 class="cas-sub">Off-the-shelf specialist drafters: a tested dead end</h3>
				<p>
					Five <strong>off-the-shelf, released checkpoints</strong> scored across four topics, each
					against the same target output. No fine-tuning, distillation or alignment toward the target
					was performed on any of them. The same-size <strong>Qwen2.5-Coder</strong> specialist merely
					<strong> ties</strong> the Qwen2.5 generalist on code; the <strong>Qwen2.5-Math</strong>
					specialist is <strong>worse</strong> than the same-size Qwen2.5 generalist even on math. The
					best possible router over this set would pick the generalist 1.5B everywhere. Acceptance is
					about matching the <em>target's own style</em>, and a same-family generalist matches a
					generalist target better than a narrow expert does.
					<strong> Bigger helps; picking an off-the-shelf specialist doesn't.</strong>
				</p>
				<p class="cas-note">
