			<section class="cas-block cas-takeaway">
				<h2>What it means</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>The claim.</strong> Before any draft compute is spent, the target has already cached a
						state that carries a calibrated, causally load-bearing signal about whether the next round of
						speculation will connect. It survives a baseline of entropy, margin, history and domain; it
						replicates across two model families and two corpora; and it held under frozen-test
						discipline with the strict protocol scored exactly once.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Its bounds, stated precisely.</strong> First token only, not run length. It adds to
						within-round control rather than replacing it. It changes real decisions only where drafting
						is expensive; elsewhere it is a diagnostic. We claim no more than that, and no wall-clock
						gain at all until the systems gate runs.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Negatives that do work.</strong> Draft-side states don't beat free surface signals,
						cheap compressions of the frontier state lose most of the gain, and off-the-shelf
						topic-specialist drafters don't beat a same-size generalist. Each one narrows where the
						remaining headroom actually is.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>One result stands with no appeal to internal states.</strong> Acceptance is shaped by
						token category: structural tokens beat entity tokens by +0.12 to +0.21 in all 15 domain
						cells, against criteria registered before unblinding. At the grain tested, it is
						<em> not</em> shaped by generation phase, and we report that failed half too.
					</li>
				</ul>
